==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|Muhammad (sura)}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|ar|مُحَمَّد||[[praise]]d, [[commendable]], [[laudable]]}}, the passive participle of {{m|ar|حَمَّدَ||to [[praise]], [[commend]], [[laud]], [[extol]]}}. From the same triconsonantal root {{ar-root|ح|م|د|nocat=1}} as, but distinct from, {{m|en|Mahmud}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-Muhammad.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|s}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[figure]] who [[introduce]]d [[Islam]], the man to whom the [[Qur'an]] was [[reveal]]ed; considered a prophet by Muslims and [[Bahá'í]]s.
#: ''Shias believe that Ali succeeded the prophet '''Muhammad''' as leader of the Ummah.''
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The 47th [[sura]] (chapter) of the [[Qur'an]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} {{given name|en|male|from=Arabic}} very popular among [[Muslim]]s.
#: ''There are two '''Muhammads''' in my class.''

====Alternative forms====
* ''[[Mahomet]]'' was the predominant form until the late 1700s. ''[[Mohammed]]'' was the predominant form from then until the 1940s, when it was overtaken by ''Muhammad'', which has been predominant ever since. Other forms which are now quite common are ''[[Mohammad]]'', ''[[Mohamed]]''{{,}} and ''[[Muhammed]]'', which all came into common use after the 1820s.
